@startuml
!include https://raw.githubusercontent.com/proveo-ca/identity/main/proveo.puml
skinparam noteTextAlignment left
' purpose: why the posture is one value rendered twice, and why nothing here decides anything
' covers: internal/posture/posture.go
' covers: cmd/proveo/posture_golden_test.go
' covers: internal/posture/posture_test.go
'
' ── THE BUG THAT MADE IT A PACKAGE ─────────────────────────────────────────
' A run states its posture twice: the header a human reads BEFORE launch, and the
' run-log block a human reads AFTER a failure. Those were assembled independently,
' so they could disagree about the same run — and did: a row named Squid logs on a
' backend that runs no Squid. Two renderings of one value cannot disagree; two
' assemblies of the same idea always eventually do.
'
' ── NOTHING HERE DECIDES ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
' Every input arrives as a VALUE. Posture never reads env, never probes docker,
' never consults a manifest. That is the whole discipline: the moment a row can
' work out its own answer, the row becomes a second place the decision is made,
' and the header stops being a report of the run and starts being a claim about
' it. MCPGateway and ResolveImageChoice take what they need as arguments for
' exactly this reason. Observability and EnforcedBy take nothing since
' 2026-10-16: with one backend their answer is a constant, not a decision.
'
' ── 2026-10-16: ONE BACKEND, FOURTEEN ROWS (MEASURED) ──────────────────────
' The docker+egress backend is deleted ("Retire Docker egress"). The rows that
' described its tier — "egress tier", "brokered", "reachable hosts", "harness
' hosts" — are gone, and Render writes 14 rows
' (TestPostureRendersEveryRowItDeclares). Their values now read:
'   credentials    "broker (sbx proxy)", or "forward (the key rides the sandbox
'                  env)" under `--credentials forward` (run.credentialsPosture,
'                  MEASURED at the last read; it read "broker" unconditionally
'                  earlier the same day)
'   enforced by    "sbx — the host's network policy + credential proxy"
'   observability  "sbx/policy-log.json — the daemon's allowed/blocked hosts
'                  (no MITM, so no DLP or body scan)"
'
' ── WHAT THE GOLDENS PIN ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
' cmd/proveo/posture_golden_test.go renders the posture and pins the bytes:
' one golden, posture-sbx. HISTORY: there was one golden per backend, because
' the wording differed on purpose ("proveo runs no Squid or MITM" against
' "squid + mitmproxy sidecars"); posture-docker.golden went with the backend.
'
' The block is what an operator reads AFTER a failure, and until the goldens
' landed NOTHING asserted it. Order was never the exposure — runlog.Log.Fields
' sorts the keys, so it was already stable. The SET and the WORDING were: a row
' could appear, vanish or change its sentence and no test would notice.
'
' The goldens pin bytes; one property is pinned separately and is the reason the
' split was made. Every row the block reports is NON-EMPTY for a fully-resolved
' run, so a field added to Posture and left unset fails in the suite rather than
' printing "(unset)" to an operator mid-incident.
'
' ── RESOLVED 2026-10-16: THE ENFORCED-BY OVERSTATEMENT ─────────────────────
' EnforcedBy said "sbx — Kit network allowlist + credential proxy" whatever sbx's
' global baseline was; on an allow-all host the Kit allowlist ADDS reach, so the
' row overstated. It now names "the host's network policy", which is what
' decides (_spec/internal/sbx/policy-baseline.puml).
title internal/posture — one value, two readers, no decisions

participant "run.buildPosture" as RUN <<app>>
participant "posture.Posture" as P <<data>>
participant "header\n(before launch)" as H <<app>>
participant "run log\n(after failure)" as L <<data>>

RUN ARROW_MAIN P : every field as a VALUE
P ARROW_MAIN H : Render()
P ARROW_MAIN L : Fields()

note over P
  **A row may not compute its answer.** Give it
  the value. A row that can decide is a second
  place the decision is made, and the two readers
  drift apart again.
end note
@enduml
